      return `<section class="tt-card tt-intro"><p class="tt-eyebrow">${remedial?"REVIEW BEFORE TRYING AGAIN":"A DECISION-MAKING GAME"}</p><h2>Imagine you are a tutor.</h2><p class="tt-task-gist">Imagine you are a tutor helping two students improve their academic performance. In each round, you decide whether to ask questions about how much tutoring they need, then divide the time left between them to achieve the highest combined result. Each question uses time that would otherwise be available for tutoring, so more information leaves less time to teach.</p>${remedial?`<div class="tt-note tt-warning"><p>Attempt ${last.attempt} of 3 did not pass the first understanding check. Review the points below and the display walkthrough before trying again.</p>${last.missed.map(i=>`<p>${esc(qs[i].explanation)}</p>`).join("")}</div>`:""}<h3>Your goal</h3><p>To see how well your tutoring worked, you will see the students' final grade point averages. Grade point average, or <strong>GPA</strong>, summarizes academic performance; higher is better. Your goal is the <strong>highest sum of the two final GPAs</strong>. Add the two GPAs to find that sum.</p><p>In this story, the students' parents hire you for a fixed number of minutes and pay the tutor a larger bonus for a higher combined GPA. Both students begin with a GPA of <strong>3.50</strong>, so their starting sum is <strong>7.00</strong>.</p><h3>What you are deciding</h3><p>To decide how to divide your time, you need to consider how much help each student needs. A student's <strong>tutoring need</strong> is the amount of tutoring they need to keep their GPA at 3.50. Exactly that much keeps it unchanged; less lowers it and more raises it.</p><p>The screen gives a <strong>current guess</strong> of each student's tutoring need and a range showing how uncertain it is. A guess can be wrong. The walkthrough on the next page explains these parts of the display.</p><div class="tt-steps"><div><span>1</span><h3>Look at the information</h3><p>Compare the students' current guesses and ranges, the time left and how much time a question uses.</p></div><div><span>2</span><h3>Choose whether to ask</h3><p>You can ask either student to explain a concept. Their answer gives an imperfect clue about their need, and the screen updates the current guess and range. You only listen; asking does not teach them or change their actual need.</p></div><div><span>3</span><h3>Stop and divide the time</h3><p>When you stop asking, use a slider to choose one student's tutoring time. The other receives the rest. You can adjust the slider before confirming your allocation.</p></div></div><h3>Information uses tutoring time</h3><p>Each question takes time out of the shared tutoring budget. For example, with 60 minutes available and 3 minutes per question, asking twice leaves 54 minutes for tutoring: 60 − 3 − 3 = 54.</p><p>You may stop immediately or keep asking while enough time remains. Questions take the same time for either student, and there is no separate question-count limit. Reading, deciding, moving the slider and reviewing the GPA rule do not use the tutoring budget.</p><p>In practice, selecting stop opens a confirmation screen; confirming ends asking. In main rounds, selecting <strong>Stop asking and divide the time</strong> takes you directly to the slider and ends asking immediately. Once asking has ended, you cannot ask again in that round.</p><p>To interpret new answers, remember that each student's actual need stays fixed within a round even when their answers differ. Needs and answers are whole numbers from 1 to 100 minutes. The current guess uses all the information so far, not only the latest answer.</p><p>To understand how tutoring changes GPA, use these examples:</p>${r9Rule()}<h3>What happens next</h3><p>You will complete <strong>14 rounds: 4 practice and 10 main rounds.</strong> You can take a break halfway through the main rounds. Each round has two new fictional students; their names and subject change, but the GPA rule stays the same. After each round, you see their actual needs and final GPAs.</p><p>First, a walkthrough explains the display, followed by questions about the rules and display. After practice, a second set checks how you interpret the display before main rounds. Answer every question correctly to pass each set. <strong>You have up to 3 attempts at each set.</strong> After attempts 1 and 2, you can review the relevant explanation and retry. If you do not pass a set within 3 attempts, your participation in the task ends. These checks concern the rules, not your strategy.</p><p>If you leave this page and return to the same saved session, you can continue where it resumes. Do not start again to replace earlier attempts.</p>${button(remedial?"Review the display walkthrough":"Show me the display walkthrough","beginChecks","tt-primary")}</section>`;
